WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.700
Cleanup and destruction are lifetime transitions.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.080
They are not just housekeeping at the end of a function.

3
00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:15.900
A destroy routine may release memory, close resources, invalidate members, clear ownership, and make future use of an object invalid.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.120
That transition should be deliberate and documented.

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:26.520
Destroy functions should free only resources owned by the object or component they are destroying.

6
00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.780
They should not release borrowed data or memory owned by another layer.

7
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:38.720
If a destroy function also clears pointers, unregisters callbacks, or detaches from containers, those side effects should be part of the contract.

8
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.880
Partial initialization needs selective teardown.

9
00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:49.500
If construction fails after some members have been initialized, cleanup should release those members and leave uninitialized members alone.

10
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.480
This is easiest when fields start in known states and construction progress is tracked clearly.

11
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.160
Error paths should leave predictable state.

12
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:04.060
A failed function should define whether output pointers are unchanged, null, partially initialized, or caller-owned.

13
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:09.920
It should not leave callers guessing which cleanup function to call or whether an object escaped before failure.

14
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.080
Repeated cleanup behavior must be documented.

15
00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.020
Some cleanup helpers are intentionally safe to call more than once.

16
00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.760
Others are valid only for live objects and must not be repeated.

17
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:30.260
Either design can be acceptable when stated clearly, implemented consistently, and tested in the paths the API promises to support.
